Question: How do you typically structure a week of email content for a DTC brand? Include types of emails, segmentation and how you decide what to send and when.

Answer: 
When creating direct-to-consumer content for the week, I start by segmenting the audience based on two core factors: where they are in the customer journey and how recently they’ve engaged with the brand. This allows me to craft more intentional, relevant messages that align with what that person needs to see next.

Segmentation Strategy
• Never Purchased: These customers need to be guided toward their first purchase. I focus here on highlighting bestsellers, social proof, or answering objections through education and brand storytelling.
• One-Time Buyers: This segment gets content aimed at helping them get more value from their original purchase—think cross-sells, upsells, or showing them how to upgrade their experience with complementary products.
• Loyal/Repeat Buyers: I shift to giving them early access, loyalty perks, or nudging them toward products they may not have considered yet. This is where deeper product education and new use cases come into play.
• Lapsed / Inactive: I like to segment based on how long it’s been since they’ve visited the site or purchased. The goal is to re-engage them with what’s new—product launches, improved shipping, or something they may have missed. These emails are more personal and often softer in CTA.

Email Types for the Week

A typical content week might include:
• Monday or Tuesday: A value-based email—educational content, a customer story, or a how-to guide that builds trust.
• Wednesday: A product spotlight or cross-sell offer. This targets customers mid-week when they’re more receptive to browsing and buying.
• Friday: A time-sensitive offer or new arrival that creates urgency and leads into the weekend. Great for loyal customers or subscribers with high intent.

Send Timing

I like to time sends based on when customers are most likely to casually browse and open emails:
• Before work (6:30–9:00 AM) – while they’re checking their phones in the morning
• Lunchtime (11:30 AM–1:30 PM) – when people are taking a break
• Late afternoon/evening (4:30–7:30 PM) – during commutes, sports practices, or when they’re winding down and multi-screening in front of the TV

I generally avoid Monday mornings due to inbox competition, and Saturday afternoons, which tend to have low engagement 

